# CSS Modules Responsive Review Checklist

Use this checklist when reviewing CSS Modules and responsive UI changes.

## Responsiveness

- Mobile-first default styles are present.
- Tablet and desktop media queries only add complexity when needed.
- Common widths are considered: 360px, 390px, 768px, 1024px, 1280px.
- Fixed widths are replaced with fluid alternatives where possible.
- Layout does not create horizontal scrolling.

## CSS Quality

- Class names describe role or structure.
- Repeated spacing uses parent `gap` where possible.
- CSS variables are used for repeated design values.
- Selectors are shallow and easy to override.
- `!important` is avoided.
- Global selectors are avoided unless intentionally using `:global()`.

## Layout Safety

- Flex and grid children that contain long text use `min-width: 0` where needed.
- Images use `display: block`, `max-width` or `width: 100%`, and correct `object-fit`.
- Cards and media areas use `aspect-ratio` instead of fragile fixed heights when possible.

## Accessibility

- Focus-visible styles exist for interactive elements.
- Touch targets are large enough.
- Text remains readable on mobile.
- Visual order does not break meaningful reading order.

## Maintainability

- CSS Module file is scoped to the component.
- Component does not rely on fragile DOM depth selectors.
- Responsive behavior is documented through clear class structure, not comments only.
- No unrelated style changes are included.
